# Solving lattice gauge theories using the quantum Krylov algorithm and qubitization

**Authors:** Lewis W. Anderson, Martin Kiffner, Tom O'Leary, Jason Crain, Dieter Jaksch

**Venue:** Quantum 9, 1652 (2025); arXiv:2403.08859

**Note:** This is a STUB extraction. A full Nougat run on the 40-page PDF was
not executed for this replication (would require a GPU node for reasonable
wall time). The replication itself relied on `pdftotext -layout` output at
`../work/paper.txt` plus direct reading of the PDF equations. This stub
records the paper's section skeleton for downstream indexing.

## Abstract (paraphrased)

The authors present an end-to-end quantum-algorithmic pipeline for computing
ground-state energies of lattice gauge theories on quantum computers. The
pipeline combines (i) the Quantum Subspace Expansion (QSE) with a Krylov
basis to construct a low-dimensional effective Hamiltonian from moments of
the full Hamiltonian, and (ii) a qubitization block-encoding of the
Hamiltonian that achieves Otilde(N) gate cost, improving on prior Otilde(N^2)
LCU-based approaches. The 1+1D lattice Schwinger model (single-flavour U(1)
lattice QED) is used as the concrete test case. Classical statevector
simulation demonstrates exponential convergence of QSE in the Krylov
dimension D at small lattice sizes, and asymptotic gate-count analysis
extrapolates the resource requirements to N = 100, 1000, 10000 lattice sites.

## Section skeleton

- Section 1 — Introduction and motivation (lattice gauge theories on quantum
  computers, prior work on VQE and Trotter methods for Schwinger).
- Section 2 — The lattice Schwinger model. Kogut–Susskind Hamiltonian,
  gauge-field elimination via Gauss's law, staggered fermions.
- Section 3 — Quantum Krylov subspace expansion. Definition, moment-based
  matrix elements, generalised eigenvalue problem H c = E S c.
- Section 4 — Classical statevector simulation results. Fig. 3
  (Delta E / E_int vs D at mu=1.5, x=0.5, N up to 26). Fig. 4
  (shot-noise study, Hankel ill-conditioning, overlap vs N).
- Section 5 — Qubitization block encoding of the Schwinger Hamiltonian.
  PREPARE and SELECT circuits.
- Section 6 — Resource analysis and extrapolation. Otilde(N) T-count,
  comparison to prior LCU compilations, extrapolation to N = 100, 1000,
  10000.
- Section 7 — Discussion and outlook.
- Appendix A — Additional numerical data and derivation of the spin-only
  Hamiltonian Eq. 15.
- Appendix B — Details of the qubitization compilation.

## Key equations referenced in the replication

- **Eq. 7:** original Kogut–Susskind Hamiltonian with staggered fermions and
  gauge links.
- **Eq. 10:** the x -> 0 (strong-coupling) vacuum reference state, a single
  computational basis state (antiferromagnetic string in the spin
  representation).
- **Eq. 15:** the spin-only, gauge-eliminated, Jordan–Wigner-transformed
  Hamiltonian H = H_0 + x V. This is the operator built and diagonalised in
  `src/schwinger_krylov.py`.

## Paper parameters used in this replication

- mu = 1.5 (fermion mass)
- x = 0.5 (hopping strength; the paper's Fig. 3 target regime)
- N = 4, 6, 8, 10 (paper goes to N = 26; we stop at 10)
- D = 1..14 (Krylov dimension sweep)
